  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nSurface high pressure and upper level ridging across the eastern US \nkeeps us under moderate weather today. Our next system arrives on \nWednesday into Wednesday night that will bring a mixture of strong \nwinds in the mountains/foothills and widespread rain to the southern \nAppalachians. Mountain wave winds out of the southeast will ramp up \nthroughout the evening and continue for Wednesday. Expect the \ntypical mountain wave locations to see wind gusts of 40-55mph... \nWith a few isolated places seeing gusts above 60 in the locations \nthat typically get the strongest mountain wave winds like Cove \nMountain or Camp Creek.\n\nThe cold front that is ushering in this rain and wind will move \nthrough Wednesday into Wednesday night. The dynamics associated with \nthis frontal passage look pretty moderate... So expect there to be \nenough instability for thunderstorms, but at this time the chance \nfor severe weather is very low. Rainfall with this system is \nexpected to be more rain than we've seen in several weeks... \ngenerally between 0.25\" to 0.75\". A few locations that get multiple \nheavier thunderstorms could eclipse the 1\" threshold, especially \nalong areas of increased orographic lift on the western edge of \nridgetops, plateaus, and mountains. Can't rule out brief flooding of \nlow lying or urban areas, but with the lack of rain this Autumn, the \nrivers and streams should be able to handle the anticipated \nrainfall. \n\nTemperatures will begin to cool as we head into the weekend, and \nwe'll return to seasonal temperatures with highs in the 60s and lows \nin the 30/40s.. The drier, cooler airmass will return and another \npleasant weekend of light winds and sunny skies is expected.\n\nLonger term models show a general uptick in the synoptic pattern, so \nit looks like we might be in for a bit more active weather and rain \nchances for November compared the past several weeks.",
